WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.771
The pipeline, and what it hides.

2
00:00:02.051 --> 00:00:06.317
The quickest way to run a Hub model in Python is the transformers pipeline.

3
00:00:06.597 --> 00:00:14.256
You name a task and a model; it downloads the files, builds the tokenizer and model, and handles pre- and post-processing.

4
00:00:14.536 --> 00:00:24.712
For chat models you pass a list of messages with role and content, the same shape you use with provider APIs, and the pipeline formats them with the model’s chat template.

5
00:00:24.992 --> 00:00:35.381
The docs recommend device_map="auto" so the library places weights on the fastest device available, which on an Apple Silicon Mac is the mps backend.

6
00:00:35.661 --> 00:00:42.787
The LLM Course’s chapter 2 opens the pipeline up into three steps, and knowing them is what lets you debug.

7
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:49.403
Tokenize: the tokenizer turns text into token ids using the model’s own vocabulary.

8
00:00:49.683 --> 00:00:59.987
Model: a forward pass produces logits, one score per vocabulary entry, and generation repeats that loop token by token (Studio 1, lesson 1).

9
00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:03.552
Post-process: the ids are decoded back into text.

10
00:01:03.832 --> 00:01:15.437
Every model family has its own tokenizer, so the same sentence becomes a different number of tokens for different models, which matters for context limits and for any per-token cost comparison.
